1件在途). Total open = 89. ✓ matches prior artifact (在途 22 = 21 + 1 partial).\n\nNow let me build the scoring. Methodology (documented, reproducible):\n\n**在途 (in-transit)**: remaining event = customer receipt (签收) of open shipments.\n- Expected receipt: dispatched_at + route transit distribution from historical completed shipments on the same route (P50/P90).\n- buffer_d = days_to_promise − expected remaining days.\n- score = empirical P(transit > transit used... ) Actually better: score = P(late) = fraction of historical shipments on the same route (same transport mode bucket) whose transit days exceed (days from dispatch to promise). That's cleaner: for each open shipment, compute d2p = julianday(promise) − julianday(dispatched_at) = allowed transit days; score = P(transit_days > d2p) from the route's historical transit distribution. If d2p < 0 → already past promise → 1.0 (and overdue fact).\n- P50/P90 expected receipt = dispatched_at + route P50/P90 transit.\n- Escalation signals: 运输延误 logistics events, open exceptions, carrier_promised_at > promise.\n\nHmm, but the prior artifact used \"curve\" buckets with curve_n and base_n, and p_buffer computed against buffer_d = days_to_promise − remaining P50. That's a \"residual\" empirical distribution: distribution of (actual total transit − P50 transit) or of remaining time. Both are reasonable. The residual approach: score = P(residual > buffer_d) where residual = actual − p50 across a bucket. That's what \"curve_n\" is.\n\nI'll implement a clean version:\n- For 在途: use route-level historical transit distribution (completed shipments, received_at not null, transit = received − dispatched). score = P(transit > allowed_days) where allowed = promise − dispatch. Bucket fallback: route → transport_mode → all.\n- For 未发货: the remaining path is production completion + shipment + transit. Use historical \"order-level\" data: for completed orders, the relationship between (last node actual_end − snapshot-equivalent) ... Simpler and defensible: build an empirical model on historical completed orders: for each completed order, at the point when it was at stage X with N open nodes... that's complex.\n\nAlternative approach used before: for 未发货, estimate expected finish = current open node's planned_end + slip (empirical slip per stage & mode) + transit (route P50/P90). Then buffer = days_to_promise − expected remaining; score = empirical P(late) from a \"curve\" bucket = distribution of (actual completion+receipt − planned_end − transit) residuals.\n\nLet me think about what's simplest, defensible, and reproducible:\n\n**Empirical \"remaining-time\" curve by bucket.** For historical (already fully-signed) orders, we can reconstruct: at a given \"state\" (current stage code, fulfillment mode, days-to-promise), what was the realized remaining time to full receipt? But we can't easily reconstruct past states... Actually we can: for a completed order, at the moment of snapshot-equivalent we don't have a state. But we can use a proxy: for each completed order, define state = the stage that was open when... hmm.\n\nSimpler, standard approach: **component-wise empirical durations**.\nFor 未发货 order o:\n1. Remaining production time R_prod: from the current open node (earliest incomplete node, by sequence), estimate = sum over remaining nodes of empirical node duration (actual_end − actual_start, or actual_end − planned_start) by (stage_code, fulfillment_mode). Plus slip.\n   Better: use empirical \"node completion slip\" = actual_end − planned_end by (stage_code, fulfillment_mode). Then expected finish of last node = max(planned_end of last open node) + slip_p50.\n2. Then expected receipt = expected finish + transit (route p50/p90 from historical shipments).\n3. buffer = days_to_promise − (expected receipt − now).\n4. score = P(late) estimated empirically: use the historical distribution of (actual receipt − planned last node end − route transit p50) i.e., total residual uncertainty, and ask P(residual > buffer).\n\nThat's essentially what the prior artifact did (curve buckets a-i).\n\nGiven the complexity, and that the prior artifact already implements a coherent version, maybe the best move: rebuild a documented script implementing this methodology, run it, and compare with the artifact for sanity. If results broadly agree, deliver.\n\nBut I don't know the exact bucket definitions of the prior artifact. I'll define my own, documented, and reproducible. Let me design concretely:\n\n### Population\n- 在途 (22 orders: 21 fully-dispatched-not-received + 1 partial-dispatch): remaining = 签收 of open shipments.\n- 未发货 (67 orders): remaining = 生产/采购 → 质检 → 入库 → 发货 → 签收.\n\n### 在途 scoring\nFor each open shipment s:\n- allowed = promise − dispatched_at (days)\n- Route historical transit distribution D(route) from completed shipments (received_at not null AND dispatched ≤ snapshot).\n- If n(D) ≥ risk_min_samples(5): p_late = P(transit > allowed). Else fall back to transport_mode pool, then all shipments.\n- If allowed < 0 → p_late = 1.0 (延期事实)\n- P50/P90 expected receipt = dispatched_at + P50/P90 of D.\n- Order-level: take max over open shipments (worst) of p_late; expected receipt = latest.\n- Signals: last logistics event status (运输延误/异常), open exceptions, carrier_promised_at > promise.\n\n### 未发货 scoring\n- Current open node: earliest incomplete node by sequence (cur_stage, planned_end).\n- Expected production finish = planned_end of the LAST incomplete node + slip_p50(stage,mode) where slip = actual_end − planned_end over completed nodes.\n- Expected receipt = expected finish + transit_p50(route) (+ small ship lag? use transit from dispatch; assume dispatch at finish).\n- buffer = (promise − now) − (expected receipt − now) = promise − expected receipt (days).\n- p_late = P(residual > buffer) where residual distribution = for historical completed orders: (actual full receipt − (planned_end of last node + slip_p50 + transit_p50)) — i.e., the model error. Bucket by (fulfillment_mode, route group) or by cur_stage.\n\nHmm, this is getting heavy. Let me simplify to a defensible empirical model:\n\n**Model error distribution (calibration curve)**: For all historical orders that are now complete (680), compute:\n- pred_receipt = last incomplete... no, for complete orders use: planned_end of last node (SHIP stage node?) + transit_p50(route).\n- err = actual_receipt − pred_receipt (days).\nThen for an open 未发货 order: p_late = P(err > buffer) where buffer = promise − pred_receipt. Using the empirical distribution of err, bucketed by fulfillment_mode (and maybe cur_stage). This is a clean \"empirical residual CDF\" approach — one number per bucket, sample sizes reported.\n\nLet me check stage_nodes structure: does it include a SHIP stage? Prior SQL used stage_code='SHIP'. Let me examine stage codes and see how well planned_end predicts.\n\nLet me explore data to calibrate. First check: for completed orders, distribution of (actual full receipt − last node planned_end) by mode.\n\nLet me run some exploratory SQL.\n", "id": "d1fa40517dc34ba6bfedd16077ce1875", "created_at": "2026-09-18T00:09:46.336216", "finished_at": null}, {"type": "tool_call", "id": "call_c17b83d835e14e658498fa1b", "name": "execute_shell_command", "input": "{\"command\": \"cd . && python3 datahub/scripts/query.py \\\"SELECT stage_code, stage_name, COUNT(*) n, SUM(actual_end_at IS NOT NULL) done, MIN(planned_start_at) mn, MAX(planned_end_at) mx FROM stage_nodes GROUP BY 1,2 ORDER BY n DESC\\\" && echo \\\"=== routes ===\\\" && python3 datahub/scripts/query.py \\\"SELECT r.route_id, r.route_name, r.transport_mode, r.baseline_transit_days, COUNT(s.shipment_id) n_ship, SUM(s.received_at IS NOT NULL) n_recv FROM routes r LEFT JOIN shipments s ON s.route_id=r.route_id GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY n_ship DESC\\\"\"}", "state": "finished", "suggested_rules": [], "created_at": "2026-09-18T00:10:19.957561", "finished_at": null}, {"type": "tool_result", "id": "call_c17b83d835e14e658498fa1b", "n